          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a case where two conditions documented together must NOT be linked by the coder?",
          "answer": "Yes. The retrieved guidance describes a few situations where two conditions must not be reported together, but it does not give a full list of them.\n\n- **Excludes1 notes** (ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions): when a tabular note says \"Excludes1,\" the two conditions cannot both be present, so their codes are never reported together.\n- **Asthma** (Coding asthma: J45): status asthmaticus and acute exacerbation are alternatives, not stages. If status asthmaticus is documented, the code says that and does not also say acute exacerbation. The coder does not choose between them by judgment. It is a documentation question.\n- **Reflux disease** (Coding GERD: K21): symptoms such as heartburn or regurgitation are not coded separately when reflux disease is documented, because they are part of it. If only the symptom is documented, the symptom code is the one that applies.\n\nTwo related points from the same documents are about adding codes, not removing them. Hiatal hernia and reflux disease are different conditions, so both get codes (Coding GERD: K21). Hypertensive heart disease and heart failure are coded together, with the heart failure code added to the combination code (Coding heart failure: I50).\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover every Excludes1 pair, so I can't tell you whether a specific pair of conditions falls under that rule. For that, check the tabular list for the codes involved, since it holds the instructional notes that the index omits. A certified coder or your coding team can confirm a particular case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
